docs/arnold/workflow-manifest-runtime-review/subagent-results/wave9/w9-05-state-data-deletion-attack.txt:5:**1. Archived/projected state still readable on disk.** M4 classifies old `.megaplan` state as `archive` or `project read-only`. If surviving CLI/operator code contains residual `open()`/`json.load()` calls pointing at `.megaplan/plans/*/state.json`, those files remain the de facto authority even though M6 import/wheel gates are green. The deletion plan scans imports, not filesystem reads.
docs/arnold/workflow-manifest-runtime-review/subagent-results/wave9/w9-05-state-data-deletion-attack.txt:7:**2. Incomplete event-journal field coverage.** M3's event journal may omit fields that old `state.json` carried — gate signal history, cost accumulators, override audit trails. If `arnold workflow status` falls back to reading old state for "completeness," the old file remains authoritative. M6 golden tests verify happy-path parity, not exhaustive field coverage.
docs/arnold/workflow-manifest-runtime-review/subagent-results/wave9/w9-05-state-data-deletion-attack.txt:11:**4. Lock files surviving as coordination gatekeepers.** Stale `.megaplan/plans/*/lock` files with old PIDs/TTLs. If surviving orchestration code still checks these locks (or if lock cleanup is classified as `ignore`), the system remains dependent on old lock state for correctness. M6 gates don't test lock resolution.
docs/arnold/workflow-manifest-runtime-review/subagent-results/wave9/w9-05-state-data-deletion-attack.txt:25:| 1 | **Filesystem-read audit gate**: Scan all surviving code for any read of `.megaplan/` paths (`open`, `Path.read_*`, `json.load`, `yaml.safe_load`, `os.listdir`, `glob`). Any such access must trace to explicit migration/projection code with a hard sunset flag that M6 verifies as expired, or be blocked. | Add to M6 Done Criteria |
docs/arnold/workflow-manifest-runtime-review/subagent-results/wave9/w9-05-state-data-deletion-attack.txt:29:| 5 | **Lock-file cleanup gate**: All lock files under `.megaplan` deleted or archived before M6 completes. Surviving lock-checking code must not read old lock paths. | Add to M6 Done Criteria |
docs/arnold/workflow-manifest-runtime-review/subagent-results/wave9/w9-05-state-data-deletion-attack.txt:33:| 9 | **Installed-wheel `.megaplan`-absent test**: The installed wheel must pass full Megaplan smoke (fresh plan, resume, gate iteration, tiebreaker) with `.megaplan/` either absent or containing only explicitly archived/deleted data. | Strengthen M6 Done Criteria #15 |
docs/arnold/workflow-manifest-runtime-review/subagent-results/wave9/w9-05-state-data-deletion-attack.txt:53:Only when all seven evidence artifacts are committed and green can M6 be considered truly closed. Without them, old `.megaplan` state survives as a silent authority — deletion gates pass on paper while the old files remain the real source of truth for resume, status, cost tracking, lock coordination, and operator inspection.
docs/arnold/workflow-manifest-runtime-review/subagent-results/wave2/w2-08-discovery-package-surface.txt:7:**2. Two Manifest Systems — No Coexistence or Migration Plan.** The current discovery pipeline (`arnold.pipeline.discovery.manifest`) uses a native-first manifest contract with `driver` requirements, while the workflow-manifest-runtime M1 defines a new `WorkflowManifest` v1 contract. The plan has no milestone addressing how these two manifest systems relate, whether old discovery manifests become deprecated, or how pipeline packages declare themselves under the new explicit-node regime versus the old native-first regime during the transition. This is a **discovery trust gate gap**.
docs/arnold/workflow-manifest-runtime-review/subagent-results/wave2/w2-08-discovery-package-surface.txt:13:**5. No Namespace-Package Wheel Configuration.** The plan calls `arnold_pipelines` a namespace package (M4), but `pyproject.toml` only packages `arnold` under `[tool.hatch.build.targets.wheel]`. There's no hatch config for the `arnold_pipelines` namespace. This is a **wheel packaging gap** that blocks M4 and makes M6's installed-wheel gate untestable.
docs/arnold/workflow-manifest-runtime-review/subagent-results/wave2/w2-08-discovery-package-surface.txt:15:**6. Deletion Hazard — py.typed Audit Not Milestoned.** `py.typed` markers exist at `arnold/py.typed`, `arnold/pipeline/py.typed`, `arnold/pipelines/megaplan/py.typed`, etc. M6 says "public type-surface checks prove no stale re-exports... leak deleted APIs," but no milestone actually audits py.typed markers end-to-end. After deleting public surface from `arnold.pipeline.__init__` (Stage, Edge, PipelineBuilder, etc.), the py.typed promises will be silently wrong unless regenerated or audited. The type-surface gate is named but not implemented anywhere in the plan.
docs/arnold/workflow-manifest-runtime-review/subagent-results/wave2/w2-08-discovery-package-surface.txt:19:**8. Discovery Trust Gate — `megaplan/pipelines` Path Fragment Will Break.** `arnold/pipelines/megaplan/discovery/trust.py` hardcodes `megaplan/pipelines` as a legacy in-tree path fragment. After M6 deletes old paths, this fragment may resolve to nothing or to a non-existent directory. The trust gate silently returns QUARANTINED, but no test validates post-migration trust classification.
docs/arnold/workflow-manifest-runtime-review/subagent-results/wave2/w2-08-discovery-package-surface.txt:29:| **M6** | Fix locked decision: Replace garbled `arnold.pipelines.megaplan` self-contradiction with `arnold.pipelines.megaplan` (old location) as deletion target. Add done criterion: py.typed audit gate passes (no stale type-surface promises). Add done criterion: Package-disposition.yaml validator passes against post-deletion file tree. Add: Remove `megaplan/pipelines` from discovery trust path fragments or replace with target path. |
