docs/arnold/workflow-manifest-runtime-review/subagent-results/wave1/w1-05-megaplan-one.txt:3:End-state expectation 5 is directionally correct — Megaplan must become a product pipeline under `arnold_pipelines.megaplan`, built as explicit workflow data over the manifest runner. The listed stage behaviors (prep→plan→critique→gate→revise→tiebreaker→human→finalize→execute→post-review) capture the visible pipeline. But expectation 5 is not yet the right target for M4 as written — it omits load-bearing behaviors evident in the characterization corpus.
docs/arnold/workflow-manifest-runtime-review/subagent-results/wave1/w1-05-megaplan-one.txt:9:**Override/fallback routing.** Force-proceed, abort, escalate, and fallback edges are separate primitives from model gate recommendations. The 34-scenario auto_drive corpus proves these are real: `escalate_force_proceed`, `escalate_abort`, `force_proceed_failed`, `no_next_step_no_override`, `auto_escalate_up`.
docs/arnold/workflow-manifest-runtime-review/subagent-results/wave1/w1-05-megaplan-one.txt:11:**Dynamic topology.** Robustness levels change graph shape — reviewer counts, loop caps, optional prep/feedback paths. `iteration_cap`, `blocked_retry_quality_to_cap`, and `context_retry_exhausted` prove topology varies per config.
docs/arnold/workflow-manifest-runtime-review/subagent-results/wave1/w1-05-megaplan-one.txt:19:Status/trace/resume belong primarily to M3 runtime, not M4 product migration. M4 must ensure they *work through manifest events*, but parity proof for those surfaces should gate at M3, not duplicate at M4.
docs/arnold/workflow-manifest-runtime-review/subagent-results/wave1/w1-05-megaplan-one.txt:33:1. **Golden parity gate**: All 34 auto_drive + 5 supervisor replay golden scenarios pass under manifest runner (M4 done criterion 3 strengthened).
