docs/arnold/workflow-manifest-runtime-review/subagent-results/wave1/w1-10-sequencing.txt:11:**2. No integration smoke between runner and product (M3↔M4 gap).** M4's Megaplan migration is the first real consumer of M3's fake-backend runner. If M3's execution semantics don't cover a Megaplan edge case (human-gate resume, tiebreaker subpipeline retry), M4 stalls with no fallback. A thin integration gate between M3 and M4 — compile and fake-run the canonical Megaplan shape before migrating product code — would catch this early.
docs/arnold/workflow-manifest-runtime-review/subagent-results/wave1/w1-10-sequencing.txt:13:**3. M5 is four workstreams in one milestone.** Shipped-pipeline migration, CLI commands, docs/scaffolds, and inventory scanning are bundled. The inventory (which feeds M6's deletion list) shouldn't gate on completing all pipeline migrations — it should begin as soon as M4 lands, in parallel with M5a pipeline work.
docs/arnold/workflow-manifest-runtime-review/subagent-results/wave1/w1-10-sequencing.txt:30:| **M3** | Add done criterion: "Canonical Megaplan shape from M2 compiles and fake-runs end-to-end." This becomes the M3→M4 gate. |
docs/arnold/workflow-manifest-runtime-review/subagent-results/wave1/w1-10-sequencing.txt:33:| **M6** | Add incremental gate: require M4 deprecation warnings to have been visible for one milestone before deletion. No pure-binary cutover. |
docs/arnold/workflow-manifest-runtime-review/subagent-results/wave1/w1-10-sequencing.txt:39:1. **M1b→M2 gate:** M1 manifest compiles a minimal branch+loop+fanout+subpipeline shape (hand-authored JSON) before M2 starts.
docs/arnold/workflow-manifest-runtime-review/subagent-results/wave1/w1-10-sequencing.txt:40:2. **M3→M4 gate:** M2's canonical Megaplan test manifest fake-runs through M3 runner with all node types exercised.
docs/arnold/workflow-manifest-runtime-review/subagent-results/wave1/w1-10-sequencing.txt:41:3. **M4→M5a gate:** Golden parity passes AND dual-run drift report shows zero un-explained diffs across 10 planning runs.
docs/arnold/workflow-manifest-runtime-review/subagent-results/wave1/w1-10-sequencing.txt:42:4. **M5b→M6 gate:** Inventory scanner returns zero unresolved shipped-pipeline blockers AND deprecation warnings have fired since M4.
docs/arnold/workflow-manifest-runtime-review/subagent-results/wave1/w1-10-sequencing.txt:44:**Bottom line:** The sequence is right; the seams between milestones are the risk. Add integration gates, split M1 and M5, and mandate a deprecation visibility period before M6 deletion.
